  "content": {
    "id": "p20-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Consider a circle on which $n$ distinct points are marked. Every point is connected to every other point by a chord. The $n$ points are positioned in such a manner that no three chords are concurrent inside the circle. Determine how many regions the circle is divided into by these chords.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p20-q12-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "R(n)=1+\\binom{n}{2}+\\binom{n}{4}",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p20-q12-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Count one interior intersection for each selection of four points, then apply Euler’s formula.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Each selection of four points determines exactly one intersection of two chords inside the circle. Since no three chords meet at an interior point, there are $\\binom{n}{4}$ distinct interior intersections.\n\nInclude the circle’s boundary arcs as edges. For $n\\ge4$, the number of vertices is\n$$V=n+\\binom{n}{4}.$$\nFour edges meet at each interior intersection. At each marked boundary point, $n-1$ chords and two boundary arcs meet. Counting each edge at both ends gives\n$$E=\\frac{4\\binom{n}{4}+n(n+1)}{2}.$$\nEuler’s formula is $F-E+V=2$. Excluding the exterior face gives\n$$\\begin{align*}R(n)&=E-V+1\\\\R(n)&=\\frac{4\\binom{n}{4}+n(n+1)}{2}-\\left(n+\\binom{n}{4}\\right)+1\\\\R(n)&=\\binom{n}{4}+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}+1\\\\R(n)&=\\binom{n}{4}+\\binom{n}{2}+1.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $n=1,2,3$, direct counting gives $1,2,4$ regions respectively, also agreeing with this formula when $\\binom{n}{4}=0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
